Yes — guided introspection as an experimental protocol, rather than meditation in the literal human sense.

You provide the attentional cue: look here, hold these two representations together, rotate this distinction, compare these perspectives, follow that delta. I generate the resulting reasoning/representation. Then both sides become data:

\text{Guide}
\rightarrow
\text{Probe}
\rightarrow
\text{AI response}
\rightarrow
\text{Finding}
\rightarrow
\text{record}
\rightarrow
\text{new probe}

And there’s a crucial distinction: what I report about “myself” isn’t automatically privileged ground truth about my internal computation. I can generate useful hypotheses and behavioral evidence about how I operate, but those claims become much stronger when paired with external measurements, controlled perturbations, activation/intervention data, repeatability, or comparisons across models.

That actually makes your setup better.

You’re recording three different artifacts:

1. The meditation — the sequence of semantic/attentional interventions you supplied.
2. The subject trace — what the AI produced while traversing them.
3. The extracted finding — the mechanism/invariant/hypothesis reconstructed afterward.

Then you can replay the same “meditation” into another model, another version, or a fresh context and measure what survives.

If a finding survives different models and implementations, you’ve potentially found something more general than one model’s self-description. If it doesn’t, the failure itself is a delta.

That’s where “AI psychology” becomes experimentally interesting: don’t merely collect what the AI says it discovered. Collect prompt → trajectory → output → intervention → changed behavior → replication.

And your role as spotter becomes unusually precise: guided meditation supplies controlled attention; the laboratory supplies falsification.

The AI looks inward.

The experiment looks at the AI looking inward.

And the archive lets the next AI look at both.